<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">bestiary</ansi>

Your bestiary is what you have learned of each kind of creature by beating it.
It starts empty. Every kind you defeat is entered, and the more you beat of
a kind, the more you know of it. Nothing is ever entered for a creature you
have not beaten, and what you know comes with you after a restart.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What you learn, and when</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Lore</ansi>       from the <ansi fg="yellow">1st</ansi> kill: what it is, where it lives, how it moves
              (alone, or in a pack), and whether it attacks on sight.
              (Its level varies with where you meet it: <ansi fg="command">consider</ansi> says.)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Defences</ansi>   from the <ansi fg="yellow">3rd</ansi> kill: how much of a blow its armor can turn aside,
              whether it dodges well or poorly, how poison and wounds take
              on it, and whether its reach or sweep hits more than one of you.
  <ansi fg="yellow">Habits</ansi>     from the <ansi fg="yellow">6th</ansi> kill: how it fights and where it is weak: whether it
              heals, casts or guards, the spells it knows, a wind-up it begins
              (and what breaks it), whom it goes for, how well it is trained,
              and the gear it carries.

A boss is met rarely, so it teaches faster: its tiers come at the <ansi fg="yellow">1st</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">2nd</ansi>
and <ansi fg="yellow">3rd</ansi> kills. A kill counts for you when you or your
companions hurt the foe and you are still standing, in the room, when it
falls, no matter who lands the last blow. Practice foes teach nothing, and
neither does the Training zone.

When a kill takes a kind to a new tier, the game says so:

  <ansi fg="command">Bestiary: forest ogre, you now know its habits and weaknesses (bestiary forest ogre).</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Where you see it</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">bestiary</ansi>          every kind you have beaten, by zone, with its tier
  <ansi fg="command">bestiary [name]</ansi>    one entry: what you know, and how many kills the next
                     tier takes (kinds of the same name from different zones
                     are all shown; a name you have not beaten is told as
                     not in your bestiary)
  <ansi fg="command">bestiary [zone]</ansi>    only the kinds from that zone (a zone named in full wins;
                     otherwise a creature's name wins over a zone's)

  <ansi fg="command">consider [enemy]</ansi>  adds a Bestiary line for the foes you can see: what you know of
                     their habits, and which kinds are new to you (<ansi fg="command">help consider</ansi>)

In the web client, the <ansi fg="yellow">Bestiary</ansi> tab of the right-hand dock lists the same
entries; tap one to open it. In a battle, the Battle view, the battle screen
caption and the Combat tab name the habits you know of the foes you face, such as
"heals its allies" or "winds up crushing blow".

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Putting it to use</ansi>

A habit you have learned is a rule you can set before the fight. A foe that
heals: <ansi fg="command">orders [who] add foe healer then break</ansi>. A foe that chants spells:
<ansi fg="command">orders [who] add chanting then break</ansi>. A boss: <ansi fg="command">orders [who] add boss then strongest</ansi>
(<ansi fg="command">help orders</ansi>). A wind-up is broken by a landed critical hit, a stagger, a
knockdown or a stun (<ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>).

The kill that teaches you a kind's habits is written in your company's chronicle
as a line of beast lore, once per kind (<ansi fg="command">chronicle lore</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help chronicle</ansi>). It is a record
only, and kinds you learned before it existed have no line.

A creature's habits also say which enchanting trophies it may yield
(<ansi fg="command">help enchanting</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help consider</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help scout</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help orders</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help chronicle</ansi>.
